        "text": "Continual Learning - The techniques developed for continual learning can be broadly divided into three categories: architectural (Goodfellow et al., 2014; 2013), regularization (Smith et al., 2022; Kirkpatrick et al., 2017; Zenke et al., 2017; Aljundi et al., 2018), and rehearsal (Lange et al., 2021; Hsu et al., 2018). Many of these approaches have used the formation of sparse subnetworks for continual learning (Abbasi et al., 2022; Aljundi et al., 2019b; Ramasesh et al., 2022; Iyer et al., 2022; Xu & Zhu, 2018; Mallya & Lazebnik, 2018; Schwarz et al., 2021; Smith et al., 2022).2 However, in contrast to our \u201corganic\u201d approach, these methods employ complex algorithms and additional memory consumption to explicitly determine and protect from updates model weights important for previous tasks.3\nWorks applying the Top-K activation to continual learning include (Aljundi et al., 2019b; Gozel & Gerstner, 2021; Iyer et al., 2022). Srivastava et al. (2013) used a local version of Top-K, defining disjoint subsets of neurons in each layer and applying Top-K locally to each. However, this was only used on a simple task-incremental two-split MNIST task and without any of the additional SDM modifications that we found crucial to our strong performance (Table 2).\nThe Top-K activation function has also been applied more broadly in deep learning (Makhzani & Frey, 2014; Ahmad & Scheinkman, 2019; Sengupta et al., 2018; Gozel & Gerstner, 2021; Aljundi et al., 2019b). Top-K converges not only with the connectivity of many brain regions that utilize inhibitory interneurons but also with existing results showing that Top-K has a number of advantages beyond continual learning, including: greater interpretability (Makhzani & Frey, 2014; Krotov & Hopfield, 2019; Grinberg et al., 2019), robustness to adversarial attacks (Paiton et al., 2020; Krotov & Hopfield, 2018; Iyer et al., 2022), efficient sparse computations (Ahmad & Scheinkman, 2019), tiling of the data manifold (Sengupta et al., 2018), and implementation with local Hebbian learning rules (Gozel & Gerstner, 2021; Sengupta et al., 2018; Krotov & Hopfield, 2019; Ryali et al., 2020; Liang et al., 2020).\nFlyModel - The most closely related method to ours is a model of the Drosophila Mushroom Body circuitry that obtains strong continual learning performance (Shen et al., 2021). This model, referred to as \u201cFlyModel\u201d, unknowingly implemented the SDM algorithm, specifically the Hyperplane variant (Jaeckel, 1989a) that uses random, fixed neuron addresses that are highly sparse. The only modification to the original SDM algorithm is a Top-K activation function that we also use and will justify (Keeler, 1988).\nThe FlyModel uses a random, fixed weight matrix for its first layer that stays true to the original SDM formulation. We instead learn this weight matrix and modify SDM to give it this capability. While modelling the data very differently, both approaches bolster the main message of this paper, i.e., that SDM is a continual learner. Other work is related to ours in learning the data manifold structure using local plasticity rules again with a Mushroom Body inspired architecture; however, it has not been applied to continual learning (Ryali et al., 2020; Liang et al., 2020).\n2Even without sparsity or the Top-K activation function, pretraining models can still lead to the formation of subnetworks, which translates into better continual learning performance as found in Ramasesh et al. (2022).\n3Memory replay methods will indirectly determine and protect model weights through deciding what memories to replay."
